\begin_layout Section*
Problem 4.8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a population regression with two explanatory variables, but where
 they have an interactive effect and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 appears as a quadratic:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y|x_{1},x_{2})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{2}+\beta_{3}x_{1}x_{2}+\beta_{4}x_{2}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}\equiv E(x_{1})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}\equiv E(x_{2})$
\end_inset

 be the population means of the explanatory variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 denote the average partial effect (across the distribution of the explanatory
 varaibles) of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 be the same for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Find 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 in terms of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Rewrite the regression function so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 appear directly.
 (Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

 will also appear.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Given a random sample, what regression would you run to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 directly? What if you do not know 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Apply part c to the data in NLS80.RAW, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\log(wage)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=educ$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=exper$
\end_inset

.
 (You will have to plug in the samle averages of 
\emph on
educ
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
exper
\emph default
.) Compare coefficients and standard errors when the interaction term is
 
\begin_inset Formula $educ\cdot exper$
\end_inset

 instead, and discuss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is an essentially continuous variable, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial E(y|x_{1},x_{2})}{\partial x_{1}}=\beta_{1}+\beta_{3}x_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 actually depends on the level of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 However, we can average equation (1) across the population distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the average partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{1}=E(\beta_{1}+\beta_{3}x_{2})=\beta_{1}+\beta_{3}\mu_{2}
\]

\end_inset

Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is an essentially continuous variable, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial E(y|x_{1},x_{2})}{\partial x_{2}}=\beta_{2}+\beta_{3}x_{1}+2\beta_{4}x_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 actually depends on the level of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and itself.
 However, we can average equation (2) across the population distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the average partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{2}=E(\beta_{2}+\beta_{3}x_{1}+2\beta_{4}x_{2})=\beta_{2}+\beta_{3}\mu_{1}+2\beta_{4}\mu_{2}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\series default
\bar default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y|x_{1},x_{2}) & = & \beta_{0}+(\beta_{1}+\beta_{3}\mu_{2})x_{1}-\beta_{3}\mu_{2}x_{1}+(\beta_{2}+\beta_{3}\mu_{1}+2\beta_{4}\mu_{2})\\
 &  & -\beta_{3}\mu_{1}x_{2}-2\beta_{4}\mu_{2}x_{2}+\beta_{3}x_{1}x_{3}+\beta_{4}x_{2}^{2}\\
E(y|x_{1},x_{2}) & = & \beta_{0}+\left(\alpha_{1}-\beta_{3}\mu_{2}\right)x_{1}+\left(\alpha_{2}-\beta_{3}\mu_{1}-2\beta_{4}\mu_{2}\right)x_{2}+\beta_{3}x_{1}x_{2}+\beta_{4}x_{2}^{2}\\
E(y|x_{1},x_{2}) & = & \beta_{0}+\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}+\beta_{3}\left[x_{1}x_{2}-\mu_{2}x_{1}-\mu_{1}x_{2}\right]+\beta_{4}\left[x_{2}^{2}-2\mu_{2}x_{2}\right]\\
E(y|x_{1},x_{2}) & = & \beta_{0}+\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}+\beta_{3}\left[x_{1}x_{2}-\mu_{2}x_{1}-\mu_{1}x_{2}+\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\right]\\
 &  & +\beta_{4}\left[x_{2}^{2}-2\mu_{2}x_{2}+\mu_{2}^{2}\right]+\left[-\beta_{3}\mu_{1}\mu_{2}-\beta_{4}\mu_{2}^{2}\right]\\
E(y|x_{1},x_{2}) & = & \left(\beta_{0}-\beta_{3}\mu_{1}\mu_{2}-\beta_{4}\mu_{2}^{2}\right)+\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}\\
 &  & +\beta_{3}(x_{1}-\mu_{1})(x_{2}-\mu_{2})+\beta_{4}(x_{2}-\mu_{2})^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w=x_{1}x_{2}-\mu_{2}x_{1}-\mu_{1}x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z=x_{2}^{2}-2\mu_{2}x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the model can be written as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y|x_{1},x_{2},w,z)=\beta_{0}+\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}+\beta_{3}w+\beta_{4}z
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since the conditional expectation is linear, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x_{1},x_{2},w,z)=0$
\end_inset

.
 So, I would run a simple OLS regression to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 directly.
 If I do not know 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

, then I will simply replace them with their sample averages, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{1}\equiv\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{2}\equiv\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I downloaded the data in NLS80 into STATA, and then I exported the data
 for 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\log(\mbox{\emph{wage}})$
\end_inset

, 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{educ}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exper}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{educ}}\cdot\mbox{\emph{exper}}$
\end_inset

, and
\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exper}}^{2}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 into a csv file.
 I used MATLAB to run an OLS regression to estimate the model we obtained
 in part C, i.e., equation (3), as well as the original model given in the
 problem outline.
 The code for these regressions can be found at the end of the assignment.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=\mbox{\emph{educ}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=\mbox{\emph{exper}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=.05$
\end_inset

, and the heteroskedasticy-robust standard errors have been calculated.
\end_layout

